// Dual-axis solar tracker: 4 LDR light sensors + 2 servo motors (Arduino Uno + Sensor Shield)
// Reconstructed tracking logic written for the pin layout in the kit manual.
// Sensors sit in four quadrants above the panel:
//   TL (A0)  TR (A1)
//   BL (A2)  BR (A3)
// Each LDR module outputs an analog value on its S pin.

#include <Servo.h>

Servo servoH;  // horizontal axis (pan)
Servo servoV;  // vertical axis (tilt)

const int PIN_SERVO_H = 5;   // horizontal servo (signal pin D5)
const int PIN_SERVO_V = 9;   // vertical servo (signal pin D9)

const int SENS_TL = A0;
const int SENS_TR = A1;
const int SENS_BL = A2;
const int SENS_BR = A3;

// --- Settings to calibrate on the real hardware ---
const int TOLERANCE  = 30;   // ignore small differences (0-1023 scale)
const int MIN_LIGHT  = 300;  // average below this = not enough sunlight, hold position
const int STEP       = 1;    // degrees moved per loop
const int LOOP_DELAY = 30;   // ms between updates (smaller = faster tracking)

// Set to -1 if a servo moves away from the light instead of toward it
// (also check the readings: if brighter light gives LOWER values, invert the comparisons)
const int DIR_H = 1;
const int DIR_V = 1;

const bool DEBUG = false;  // set true to print sensor values to the Serial Monitor

int posH = 90;  // start in the middle of the 0-180 range
int posV = 90;

void setup() {
  if (DEBUG) Serial.begin(9600);
  servoH.attach(PIN_SERVO_H);
  servoV.attach(PIN_SERVO_V);
  servoH.write(posH);
  servoV.write(posV);
}

void loop() {
  int tl = analogRead(SENS_TL);
  int tr = analogRead(SENS_TR);
  int bl = analogRead(SENS_BL);
  int br = analogRead(SENS_BR);

  int average = (tl + tr + bl + br) / 4;

  if (DEBUG) {
    Serial.print(tl); Serial.print(' '); Serial.print(tr); Serial.print(' ');
    Serial.print(bl); Serial.print(' '); Serial.println(br);
  }

  // Only react when there is strong light (sun), not dim room light
  if (average > MIN_LIGHT) {
    int top    = (tl + tr) / 2;
    int bottom = (bl + br) / 2;
    int left   = (tl + bl) / 2;
    int right  = (tr + br) / 2;

    // Vertical axis: move toward the brighter half
    if (abs(top - bottom) > TOLERANCE) {
      posV += (top > bottom) ? STEP * DIR_V : -STEP * DIR_V;
    }

    // Horizontal axis: move toward the brighter half
    if (abs(left - right) > TOLERANCE) {
      posH += (right > left) ? STEP * DIR_H : -STEP * DIR_H;
    }

    // Keep both servos inside their 0-180 degree range
    posH = constrain(posH, 0, 180);
    posV = constrain(posV, 0, 180);

    servoH.write(posH);
    servoV.write(posV);
  }

# Dual-Axis Solar Tracker (Arduino Uno, kit-based)

A dual-axis solar tracker that keeps a solar panel pointed at the brightest light, assembled, wired and programmed as a student project at Bishkek Technical College (Kyrgyz State Technical University named after I. Razzakov).

The hardware comes from a commercial DIY kit ("UNO R3 Smart Solar-Powered Light-Tracking DIY Kit", bought through a Chinese marketplace). This project is about assembling the kit, understanding how each module works, and programming the control logic.

![Prototype](docs/prototype.jpg)

## What the system does

- Four LDR light sensors, mounted in four quadrants above the panel, measure where the light is strongest
- Two MG90S servo motors pan (horizontal) and tilt (vertical) the panel toward the light
- A 12 V, 1.92 W solar panel charges a two-cell Li-ion pack through a CN3795 MPPT charging module
- The Arduino and servos run from the battery pack; a DC 9-24 V adapter can charge the pack and USB 5 V can also power the board

## Hardware (from the kit)

- Arduino Uno with Sensor Shield v5
- 2 x MG90S servo motors
- 4 x LDR photoresistor modules
- 12 V 1.92 W solar panel
- CN3795 MPPT solar charging module and battery box with 2 Li-ion cells
- Laser-cut plywood frame

## Pin assignment (from the kit manual)

| Component | Arduino pin |
|---|---|
| Horizontal servo | D5 |
| Vertical servo | D9 |
| LDR top-left / top-right | A0 / A1 |
| LDR bottom-left / bottom-right | A2 / A3 |

## How the tracking logic works

1. Read the four LDR sensors.
2. Average the top, bottom, left and right pairs.
3. If the average light is above `MIN_LIGHT`, move each servo one step toward the brighter side (only when the difference exceeds `TOLERANCE`).
4. Repeat. In dim light the panel holds its position.

